 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>One or more questions for each outcome at the top of the page; Figure 1 and its quiz cover holding entries.</p>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> The plate gives no climb gradient. What must an Air Force pilot climb at, and when may they turn?</summary><p>At least 200 ft per nautical mile with all engines operating, and no turn until 400 ft above the departure end unless the procedure requires an early turn.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> Holding at 16,000 ft away from a military field, with no published speed. What is the maximum speed, and how long is the inbound leg?</summary><p>265 KIAS, and 1½ minutes inbound, because the hold is above 14,000 ft.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> Inbound you need 5° of right drift correction. What do you hold outbound?</summary><p>15° left: three times the inbound correction, the other way.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> On a non-precision approach you see the runway at the MDA, before the VDP. May you descend?</summary><p>Not yet: do not leave the MDA before the VDP, even with the runway in sight. At or after it, with the runway environment in sight and in position to land, yes.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> On an ILS you are two dots low at 500 ft above localizer minimums. Can you continue?</summary><p>Not below localizer minimums while more than one dot low. If you recapture the glide slope within one dot low or two dots high, you may continue to the DA.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> At 8 NM, half a dot of localizer took a 5° heading correction to fix. At 1 NM you see half a dot again. Same correction?</summary><p>No. The needle shows an angle, so half a dot at 1 NM is a much smaller distance off the centerline than at 8 NM. Make a smaller correction, and keep it within 5° of the wind-corrected heading.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> On final, the HSI course bar is pinned against the side of the case. What do you do?</summary><p>Full-scale localizer deflection on final: discontinue the approach.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> On a PAR final you hear nothing for five seconds. What do you do?</summary><p>Call the controller. If you cannot reestablish contact and cannot stay VFR, fly your backup approach.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> You decide to go missed a mile before the MAP. Can you turn right away?</summary><p>No: unless ATC clears otherwise, fly the approach to the MAP at or above the DA or MDA before turning.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> Your radios fail in cloud. What do you squawk, and what do you try before guard?</summary><p>Squawk 7600 and make calls in the blind. Retry the last frequency, then other published frequencies and agencies; guard is the last resort.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> The plate shows an emergency safe altitude. What does it give you?</summary><p>At least 1,000 ft of obstacle clearance (2,000 ft in designated mountainous areas) within 100 NM of the facility, for emergency use; not navigation aid reception.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> Radio failure in cloud, assigned 6,000 ft, told to expect 8,000 ft at the next fix, on a segment where the minimum is 5,000 ft. What altitude?</summary><p>6,000 ft to the fix, then 8,000 ft: the highest of the assigned, minimum and expected altitudes for each segment.</p></details>

